that happened and skip the click if it did. I need to trace through how the long press actually gets triggered and handled. The `LongPressResponder` posts a delayed runnable to `widget` that calls the long click listener, so there's no straightforward way to check if a long press is pending without diving into private fields. Let me reconsider the approach - maybe I can track the touch state directly or find a simpler signal that indicates whether the long press has been consumed. I'm realizing the timing issue—ACTION_UP fires before the long press runnable completes, so I need to defer the click action until after the long press timeout has passed. I could post a delayed click handler that runs after the long press window, and if a long press actually triggers, I cancel it. Alternatively, Android's `ViewConfiguration.getLongPressTimeout()` might give me the delay I need to replicate this behavior. a custom approach where I track the touch state myself. I'll post a delayed runnable when ACTION_DOWN happens that waits just past the long press timeout—if the finger is still down, I'll treat it as a long press and skip the click. If the finger lifts before that timeout, I'll execute the click normally. Let me code this up and test whether the long press and click handlers are properly separated. I need to think through how to actually implement this since I can't easily check whether the long press was consumed. Maybe I should use a simpler approach—checking if a drag is pending after the long press, or just deferring the click logic with a small delay to let the system process the long press first.\n\nFor ACTION_DOWN, I'll track the link position and set up a delayed check that runs just past the long press timeout to see if the view has a long press runnable scheduled, though I realize View doesn't expose a direct way to check that. Let me reconsider with a cleaner implementation strategy.\n\nActually, the core issue is that when a long press fires, the finger is still down on the link, so when ACTION_UP comes, it shouldn't trigger another click. The solution is to post the click action with a delay slightly longer than the long press timeout, so if a long press happens first, it cancels the click. I can use `postDelayed` with `ViewConfiguration.getLongPressTimeout() + 50` to ensure the click only fires if no long press occurred. The long press handler in LinkMovementMethod uses `postDelayed(..., mLongPressTimeout)`, which is the same value returned by `ViewConfiguration.getLongPressTimeout()`. I need to post the ACTION_UP handling with a delay slightly longer than the long press timeout, and if a long press occurs, it'll consume the event and prevent the click from firing. If no long press happens, the ACTION_UP will still reach the handler. Let me trace through the base class behavior more carefully—I realize `Touch.onTouchEvent(widget, buffer, event)` is already being called, so the base class is handling some of the touch logic.\n\nThe real solution is to override the movement method to check whether a long press was consumed before triggering the click action. I'll use a flag set by an `OnLongClickListener` to track this, and only execute the click if the long press wasn't already handled.\n\nFor the event handling, I'm checking the x and y coordinates against the layout to find which clickable span is under the touch point, then managing the selection and click behavior accordingly. I'm reconsidering the approach since `requestDisallowInterceptTouchEvent(true)` might interfere with parent gesture detection. The `LinkMovementMethod` doesn't implement `OnTouchListener`, so I need a different strategy—perhaps checking whether a long press was already triggered before handling the click action.\n\nI'll post a delayed runnable with a slight buffer after the long press timeout to distinguish between a long press and a normal tap. The implementation checks the action type against `MotionEvent.ACTION_UP` and `MotionEvent.ACTION_DOWN` to handle the link selection and click events appropriately. if (widget.hasOnLongClickListener()) {\n                    boolean shouldWait = true;\n                } else {\n                    link[0].onClick(widget);\n                }\n            } else {\n                Selection.removeSelection(buffer);\n                Touch.onTouchEvent(widget, buffer, event);\n                return false;\n            }\n        }\n        return Touch.onTouchEvent(widget, buffer, event);\n    }\n}\n```\n\nBut this approach is still flawed—if the OnLongClickListener isn't registered or returns false, the long press event gets consumed anyway and then the click fires unexpectedly. I need to reconsider this entirely. The real issue is distinguishing between a long press that should open a context menu versus a regular tap that should trigger the link, so I should look at how the parent class handles these events.\n\nWhen ACTION_DOWN occurs on a link, the base LinkMovementMethod selects it and schedules a long press check. If a long press fires first, it calls the OnLongClickListener and consumes the event, preventing the click from ever executing since the finger is already lifted. If no long press occurs and the finger lifts, ACTION_UP triggers the click handler. The problem arises when a custom OnLongClickListener returns true but the system still proceeds to handle the click.\n\nLooking at how Android manages this internally, there's a flag tracking whether a long press was consumed. I could try accessing it via reflection, though that's fragile. A cleaner solution is to post the click action after the long press timeout with a slight delay—if the long press fires first, it'll cancel my post and prevent the click; if no long press occurs, the click will execute as expected.\n\nBut examining the code flow more carefully, when the user lifts their finger after a long press, the ACTION_UP event still triggers the click handler even though the long press already fired.