{"type":"assistant","uuid":"3e5bddb6-11c8-4cd5-b28d-47edc38e6a30","session_id":"9e1db7f8-0f8b-4b2f-bfcf-ad6e280193c8","parent_tool_use_id":null,"message":{"id":"3e5bddb6-11c8-4cd5-b28d-47edc38e6a30","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"qwen/qwen3.6-35b-a3b","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a good understanding. Let me also check the `StoriesFragment.java` to see how the long-press listener is used, and check how the comment body handles long-press vs. click for links.\n\nThe key components are:\n\n1. **HtmlTextView** - A custom TextView that renders HTML text with clickable links\n2. **LocalLinkMovementMethod** - A custom LinkMovementMethod that handles link clicks on HtmlTextView\n3. **CommentsRecyclerViewAdapter** - Uses HtmlTextView for comment body; sets up long-click listener on both `itemView` and `commentBody`\n4. **StoryRecyclerViewAdapter** - Uses HtmlTextView in `CommentViewHolder.bodyText`\n\n\n5. **StoryRecyclerViewAdapter** - Has its own long-press handling for stories\n\nThe bug is that when long-pressing on a URL in a comment, the context menu opens but the URL also opens. This happens because `LocalLinkMovementMethod.onTouchEvent` processes both `ACTION_DOWN` and `ACTION_UP` events independently—it calls `link.onClick()` on `ACTION_UP` without checking whether a long press was already triggered and consumed.\n\nThe fix requires tracking whether a long press has been initiated. I'll set up an `OnLongClickListener` on the widget in `ACTION_DOWN`, then in `ACTION_UP` check if the long press consumed the event before calling `link.onClick()`. If the long press handler returns true, I'll cancel the click action; otherwise, I'll proceed with opening the link.\n\nActually, I realize there's a problem with this approach—if I set the listener in `ACTION_DOWN`, it might be removed when the user lifts their finger, so the check in `ACTION_UP` won't work reliably. I need to reconsider the timing of when the long press gets triggered.\n\nLooking at Android's source, the long press runnable fires after about 400ms if the finger is still down, which happens before ACTION_UP occurs. So if a long press is triggered, it should be consumed before we even reach the ACTION_UP event.\n\nThe issue is that `link[0].onClick(widget)` fires on ACTION_UP regardless of whether a long press already happened. I need to track whether a long press was consumed by maintaining a flag that gets set when the long click listener returns true, then checking that flag before triggering the click action.\n\nI'll post a delayed runnable in ACTION_DOWN that waits just past the long press timeout—if the long press was consumed, this runnable won't fire; if it wasn't, the runnable will execute and trigger the click normally.\n\nThe problem is detecting whether the long press handler actually consumed the event. I could try checking if `event.isLongPressed()`, but that's not a standard MotionEvent method. Let me reconsider the timing approach—Android's default long press timeout is around 400ms, and ACTION_UP fires after that if the user lifts their finger later, so I need a way to distinguish between a tap that triggers a click versus a hold that triggers a long press.\n\nI can post the click action with a slight delay beyond the long press timeout. If a long press occurs first, it'll cancel the post and prevent the click from firing. If no long press happens, the delayed click will execute naturally. This should handle both cases correctly.\n\nThe issue is that when ACTION_DOWN is posted, it triggers selection and returns true to keep processing events. Then ACTION_UP also returns true, but the long press runnable from the parent class might still be pending. I need to cancel any existing long press runnable before posting my delayed click action.\n\nLooking at the source, the long press runnable is stored in a private field called `mLongPressRunnable`, so I can clear it using reflection. Then I'll post my own delayed runnable